  "studyDescription": "One hundred and fifty students at a university in the southwestern United States were randomly assigned to one of two versions of a scenario about buying a $14.99 video. Both versions offered the same option (A), \"Buy this entertaining video.\" The treatment group’s option (B) added a clause reminding them that the money could be kept for other purchases; the control group’s did not. The outcome recorded was which option the student chose. This was a randomized experiment.",
  "variableDescriptions": {
    "group": "Which version of the scenario the student was randomly assigned: control, or treatment with the opportunity-cost reminder.",
    "decision": "Which option the student chose — to buy the video, or not to buy it."
  },
  "sourceDetail": "OpenIntro IMS Ch. 11; original study Frederick S, Novemsky N, Wang J, Dhar R, Nowlis S (2009), \"Opportunity cost neglect\".\n\nDIRECTION, and why it differs from the chapter (REQ-073 item 7, checked 2026-10-16). The tool counts BUYING as the success and reports control − treatment: 56/75 − 41/75 = 0.747 − 0.547 = +0.200. The course chapter (randomization-tests, § opportunity cost) counts NOT buying and reports treatment − control: 34/75 − 19/75 = 0.453 − 0.253 = +0.200. Both are correct and both give +0.200 — complementary success with the groups swapped is the same number — but they attach it to opposite sentences.\n\nAligning the tool to the chapter was ATTEMPTED and reverted: the success label is settable from this file, but the GROUP ORDER is not — `group1`/`group2` here are read by nothing, and the page derives its order from the data. Setting success to \"not buy\" alone leaves the page showing control − treatment = −0.200 against a \"greater\" alternative, which is worse than the mismatch it was meant to fix. Making the tool match the chapter needs a code change (a settable group order, or a URL parameter), not a metadata one."
}